        {
          ":db/id": 17592186148311,
          ":step/content": {
            ":db/id": 17592186148754,
            ":tutorial/content": "When the characteristic equation of a Cauchy-Euler equation has a repeated root $\\lambda = c,$ the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x) = c_1 x^c + c_2 x^c \\ln x.\n$$\n\nTo illustrate, let's solve the following Cauchy-Euler equation:\n\n$$\nx^2y''-xy'+y=0\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the trial solution $y = x^\\lambda$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda x^{\\lambda -1}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda(\\lambda-1)x^{\\lambda-2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}-x\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}+{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }-\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }(\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-\\lambda +1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation has a double root $\\lambda = 1.$ Therefore, the general solution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={c}_{1}{x}^{1}+{c}_{2}{x}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x \\\\  & ={c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
            ":tutorial/id": "2b81eee8-ed37-54cc-8162-fa06c20b8a79",
            ":tutorial/math-academy-id": 4097,
            ":tutorial/title": "Introduction"
          },
